n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186445294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186445297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01d78fae-df26-523a-a169-023a86387db3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186445293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445295 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186445294 #edb/